From ce81dbf2aa7b88c46ab12db7d8ef9b8a3ded88e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Martin <dmanlfc@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 17:02:18 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] panfrost: make Vulkan on Bifrost (Mali-G52) pass the CTS

Running the full Vulkan CTS (vk-main) on a Mali-G52 r1 MC3 (RK3576,
panfrost 1.6) without PANVK_DEBUG=sync, which is how applications use
the driver but not how CI runs it, showed crashes, flakes and GPU faults,
on top of the expected failures listed in panfrost-g52-fails.txt. This
fixes all of them.

JM backend (panvk/jm):

- Report GPU job failures. The kernel flags the fence of a job that
  faulted (-EINVAL) or was killed by the scheduler timeout (-ECANCELED),
  but gpu_queue_check_status() always returned VK_SUCCESS, so a job
  killed after panfrost's 500ms timeout silently returned garbage.
  Track the fences of submitted jobs in a merged sync_file (under a
  mutex, as check_status() runs from any waiting thread) and report
  VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST when one failed. Failed submit/wait ioctls,
  previously only caught by assert(), now mark the queue lost too.

- QueueWaitIdle() asserted the queue was not in threaded submit mode,
  which emulated timeline semaphores switch to, and returned early in
  release builds. Fall back to vk_common_QueueWaitIdle() in that case.

- Re-issuing an already issued batch reset its job headers on the CPU
  without waiting for the previous issue, so submitting a command
  buffer again while still pending zeroed job headers the GPU hadn't
  run yet (DATA_INVALID_FAULT). Wait on queue->sync first.

- Support queue global priorities: create a JM context for LOW/HIGH
  queues, and stop filtering the priorities down to MEDIUM.

- Bifrost's rectangular line rasterization drops 1px lines lying
  exactly on a pixel boundary for some viewport sizes (e.g. x = 129.0
  in a 258x258 viewport), while it otherwise picks the left/top
  column/row. Move single-sampled non-Bresenham lines up-left by one
  subpixel, within the rounding allowed when snapping to the subpixel
  grid.

- Give each VkDevice its own DRM file. A dup()ed fd shares the DRM
  file, and panfrost has one GPU address space per file, so all devices
  shared ~4GB of VA and creating many of them failed with ENOSPC.

PanVK on Bifrost:

- textureQueryLod() returned the level rounded to the nearest integer,
  which is only right for nearest mipmap filtering. Recompute it from
  the unclamped LOD and the sampler descriptor, like Valhall does.

Bifrost compiler (pan/bi):

- The scheduler lowers ATOM_RETURN.i32/i64 to *ATOM_C + +ATOM_CX, but
  bi_count_read_registers() counted ATOM_CX as reading all its staging
  registers while the pseudo-instruction reads half of them. The
  post-scheduling liveness then kept a dead register write the
  scheduler had not accounted for, putting two writes in the last tuple
  of a clause: "bi_pack_registers: Assertion `!(mode & 0x8)' failed" in
  robustness.image_robustness.*r32i*cube*. Keep the atomic opcode on
  ATOM_CX and count it like the pseudo-instruction.

With these, v7 (G31/G51/G52/G76) no longer requires
PAN_I_WANT_A_BROKEN_VULKAN_DRIVER (v6 and v14 still do).

Batocera backport to Mesa 26.2.3 of the upstream-main change, with the
src/panfrost/ci changes dropped. The full-CTS results were obtained on
Mesa main (Vulkan 1.3 on Bifrost). 26.2.3 hard-codes Vulkan 1.0 below
v10; v7 now reports 1.2, whose required features it already exposes
(1.3 needs maintenance4 and subgroup_size_control). Not CTS-validated
on 26.2.3.
